y — there is no Tier 1/Tier 2 split. The controller-runtime logr dependency is internal and already exists.\n\n---\n\nHmm wait — in the Epic List table, E004 type: \"Implementation (docs-authoring)\" — the type field is Implementation; implementation_type docs-authoring. Fine to show as note.\n\nAlso in the summary, \"Epic List (table: ID, title, type, team, priority)\". OK.\n\nOne thing: the DAG Justification \"Rule 13 (analog)\" — Rule 13 is about producing artifacts another epic builds against. My justification: lint gate requires converted codebase. Let me phrase rule as \"Rule 13 (state dependency)\" hmm. I'll say \"Rule 13 (artifact/state ordering)\". Fine.\n\n**E001 body:**\n\n# Convert rhods-operator manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured fields\n\n## Description\n\nConverts message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs and standardizes structured field names across all reconciliation error log paths in the manager binary of the rhods-operator: the DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller, 8 component controllers, the module controller with its 8 module handlers, the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller, 5 service controllers, and the 15 action files across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module). Resource name and namespace move from message strings into structured `name`/`namespace` fields; `resourceKind` is added as a structured field to all controllers; DSCI's non-standard keys (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) migrate to the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in manager-binary reconciliation error paths (grep/static analysis), producing a coverage checklist of call sites embedding resource identity in message text (the strategy's 75% figure is an estimate to be confirmed by this audit)\n- For each embedding call site: extract resource identity from the message string and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), leaving log ingestion-compatible message text\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to reconciliation error logs in: the 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup), the module controller and its 8 module handlers, the DSC controller and the 8 component controllers, and the DSCI controller\n- Migrate DSCI structured field names from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace`\n- Cover all 15 action files: 8 component (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`), 3 service (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`), 1 DSC (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`), 3 module (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`)\n- Handle cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization): `namespace` omitted or set to empty string, never a spurious value\n- Unit tests asserting structured field presence and correctness via test logger output\n- Backwards-compatibility validation: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error, and a dev-cluster test build with the production log consumer stack to verify ingestion of both formats; coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update text-based alert rules that rely on message-string patterns\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. Static analysis of the manager binary flags no remaining `log.Error` calls with resource name/namespace embedded in message text; every affected call site uses structured `name`/`namespace` key-value pairs.\n2. Grep of the codebase confirms no references to the old DSCI field names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) remain, and a unit test asserts DSCI error logs contain `name`/`namespace` with correct values.\n3. Unit tests parsing test logger output assert `resourceKind` is present with the correct value for each of the 5 service controllers.\n4. Unit tests assert `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` are present with correct values across all 15 action files in all 4 tiers.\n5. Unit tests assert module controller error logs (8 module handlers) include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`.\n6. Unit tests assert DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind`.\n7. A unit test confirms cluster-scoped resources do not emit a spurious namespace value (namespace omitted or empty string).\n8. Before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error confirms structured fields appear and the log consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes; a dev-cluster test build with the production log consumer stack shows logs ingested correctly, and text-based alert rules relying on changed message strings are identified and updates coordinated with SRE/Support.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): message-embedded identity converted to structured KV pairs — manager binary paths\n- HLR-2 (P0): DSCI key-name migration to camelCase convention\n- HLR-3 (P0): service controller error logs include resourceKind\n- HLR-4 (P0): action file error logs include name/namespace/resourceKind across 4 tiers\n- HLR-6 (P0): module controller error logs include name/namespace/resourceKind\n- HLR-7 (P1, bundled): DSC and component controller error logs include resourceKind — bundled with the P0 conversion because it edits the same log.Error call sites in the same controller files as the P0 name/namespace work; a split would double-touch the same files and duplicate the call-site audit\n\nWait — the \"no sibling epic references\" rule: mentioning HLR ids is fine (they're strategy HLRs, not sibling epics).\n\n**E002 body:**\n\n# Convert cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs to structured fields\n\n## Description\n\nApplies the same structured-logging conversion to the cloudmanager binary of the rhods-operator, which handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its three reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger; the remaining work is converting message-embedded resource identity in error paths to structured key-value pairs and adding `resourceKind`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit `log.Error()` call sites in the three cloudmanager controller reconciliation error paths\n- Convert call sites embedding resource name/namespace in message strings to structured key-value pairs\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all three controllers' reconciliation error logs (`name`/`namespace` already provided by the shared reconciler)\n- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output\n- Before/after log comparison covering cloud log consumers (e.g., CloudWatch) per the backwards-compatibility NFR\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. Static analysis flags no remaining `log.Error` calls in cloudmanager reconciliation error paths with resource identity embedded in message text.\n2. Unit tests assert `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` are present with correct values in test logger output for all three cloudmanager controllers.\n3. Before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error confirms structured fields appear and the log consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): message-embedded identity converted to structured KV pairs — cloudmanager binary paths\n- HLR-5 (P0): cloudmanager controller error logs include name/namespace/resourceKind\n\nAlso add Risks/Notes section? \"Notes: release timing (same release as manager vs. subsequent release) is an open PM scheduling question; this work is independently schedulable and deferrable — if deferred, the repo-wide CI lint enforcement is scoped to manager-binary paths until this conversion ships.\" That references the lint epic's work in plain terms — allowed (\"describe what it delivers and depends on in plain terms\"). OK, add \"## Risks / Notes\" section (additional sections allowed).\n\n**E003 body:**\n\n# CI lint enforcement for structured reconciliation log fields\n\n## Description\n\nAdds a CI lint/static-analysis rule to the rhods-operator repository that durably enforces the structured logging convention: it flags any new `log.Error` call in reconciliation error paths that embeds resource name/namespace in message text or omits the required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`). This converts the strategy's acceptance measurement (\"CI lint rule or static analysis flagging any remaining log.Error calls...\") into a permanent regression gate.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement a lint/static-analysis check (repo CI) detecting `log.Error` calls that embed resource identity in message text or lack required structured fields in reconciliation error paths, across both the manager and cloudmanager binaries\n- Wire the check into the repository's CI so it gates new changes\n- The rule lands after both binaries' conversions are complete so it passes on the converted codebase; if the cloudmanager conversion is deferred to a subsequent release per the open scheduling question, the rule is scoped to manager-binary paths until that conversion ships\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. The CI lint rule runs in the repository's CI pipeline and flags `log.Error` calls with name/namespace embedded in message text.\n2. The rule passes on the fully converted codebase (zero violations across manager and cloudmanager binaries).\n3. A deliberately introduced violation on a test branch is flagged by the rule (the gate demonstrably catches regressions).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0, enforcement aspect): provides the durable CI lint enforcement cited by HLR-1's measurement clause; the P0 conversion itself is carried by the manager-binary and cloudmanager-binary conversion work\n\nHmm — \"carried by the manager-binary and cloudmanager-binary conversion work\" — plain terms, no