{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a166d693c8a10253","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8010 — Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tNot below threshold and not docs-only. The strategy is single-component (rhods-operator) and single-implementing-team (ODH Operator team), but its scope spans architecturally distinct sub-systems (the manager and cloudmanager binaries of the rhods-operator repository) and multiple distinct work streams — mechanical code conversion, CI enforcement tooling, and substantive documentation authoring (a new contribution guideline topic with before/after examples and a migration guide) — which warrant separate PR review cycles. The strategy's own effort arithmetic (~2–2.25 sprints across audit, conversion, cloudmanager, docs + lint) also exceeds a single small epic. Full decomposition follows.\n15\t\n16\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input Overrides\n17\t\n18\tThe strategy contains a non-empty Staff Engineer / SME Input section, which takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach. Overrides applied:\n19\t\n20\t1. **Action-file inventory corrected to 15 files across 4 tiers** (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) — the \"~16 component action files\" figure was wrong and mislabeled; all 8 components have exactly one action file (8/8, confirmed by glob). Adopted in epic scope and HLR-4 coverage.\n21\t2. **Module controller tier added to scope** — the AI-generated architecture description omitted the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator), whose action files do contain error logs. Adopted (HLR-6).\n22\t3. **The \"~16 component action files\" open question is already answered** (8/8 confirmed) — no Investigation epic created for it.\n23\t4. **camelCase field naming** (`name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind`) per SME input, matching controller-runtime convention.\n24\t5. **cloudmanager uses the shared reconciler** and already receives `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger — only `resourceKind` addition and embedded-identity conversion are in scope for that binary.\n25\t\n26\t## Epic List\n27\t\n28\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n29\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Convert rhods-operator manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured fields | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Convert cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs to structured fields | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | CI lint enforcement for structured reconciliation log fields | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Document and publish the structured reconciliation log convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001[\"E001 manager binary conversion (P0)\"] --> E003[\"E003 CI lint enforcement (P1)\"]\n40\t    E002[\"E002 cloudmanager conversion (P0)\"] --> E003\n41\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 convention documentation (P1)\"]\n42\t    E002 --> E004\n43\t    E003 --> E004\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\t## DAG Justification\n47\t\n48\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n49\t|------|------|-----------|\n50\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repository, different binaries and controller areas — parallel; any overlap is merge-coordination risk, not a dependency |\n51\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (state ordering) | The lint gate's deliverable is a repo-wide CI rule that passes only on a converted codebase; activating it before the manager conversion completes would fail CI on unconverted paths |\n52\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (state ordering) | Same, for the cloudmanager binary's paths — the rule is repo-wide |\n53\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by all Implementation epics; the guideline documents what was built |\n54\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 | Same |\n55\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | Same |\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n58\t\n59\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n60\t|-----|----------|-------------|---------|-------|\n61\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All reconciliation error logs embedding resource name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | E001 covers manager-binary paths, E002 covers cloudmanager-binary paths; RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 provides the durable CI lint enforcement cited by this HLR's \"measured by\" clause |\n62\t| HLR-2 | P0 | DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n63\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n64\t| HLR-4 | P0 | All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across 4 tiers (15 files) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n65\t| HLR-5 | P0 | Cloudmanager controller (AWS/Azure/CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | |\n66\t| HLR-6 | P0 | Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n67\t| HLR-7 | P1 | DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` for cross-controller queryability | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: adding `resourceKind` to the DSC and 8 component controllers edits the same `log.Error` call sites in the same controller files as the P0 name/namespace conversion (HLR-1); a split would double-touch the same files and duplicate the call-site audit |\n68\t| HLR-8 | P1 | Convention documented as a contribution guideline and published for external operator adoption | RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | |\n69\t\n70\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n71\t\n72\t## Investigation Epic Analysis\n73\t\n74\tAll unknowns were collected from the open questions table, risks, assumptions, and the pending-review prerequisites before evaluating any of them:\n75\t\n76\t1. **\"Actual percentage of log calls embedding resource identity\" (SME says 75%)** — the audit is the first step of the conversion work; the answer changes effort, not which epics exist or what they do → folded into E001 as scope (coverage checklist) and acceptance criteria. No Investigation epic.\n77\t2. **\"Which log-based alerts/dashboards rely on message-string patterns\"** — identified during the backwards-compatibility validation that is part of the conversion work (the strategy's risk mitigation specifies before-merge dev-cluster validation with SRE/Support coordination) → acceptance criteria on the conversion epics. No Investigation epic.\n78\t3. **\"Cloudmanager same release or subsequent release\"** — a PM scheduling decision; the cloudmanager epic exists and its work is identical either way; it is independently schedulable and deferrable → noted as a scheduling risk on E002 and in Health Warnings. No Investigation epic.\n79\t4. **\"Convention fields beyond name/namespace/kind\"** — a PM/Observability decision that would extend (not restructure) the convention; the required field set is fixed by the P0 HLRs and SME input → recorded as a decision point inside the guideline epic (E004). No Investigation epic.\n80\t5. **Prerequisites (\"Architecture Review: pending human review\", etc.)** — template-level process gates applying to the whole strategy, not conditional decisions that change epic structure → Health Warning. No Investigation epic.\n81\t\n82\tNo Investigation epics: no unknown's answer changes which epics exist or what they do.\n83\t\n84\t## Health Warnings\n85\t\n86\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the manager-binary conversion epic (E001) is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's own effort estimate (call-site audit ~0.5 sprint + manager conversion ~0.75–1 sprint) — above the ~2-week guideline. Kept as one logical change: a split by controller tier would duplicate the call-site audit and fragment a single mechanical conversion over one surface. **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic by controller tier if the audit finds materially more embedding call sites than the strategy's 75% estimate implies, or if conversion effort exceeds the strategy's estimate.\n87\t- **Open decisions, not structural gates**: (a) the PM/Observability decision on optional fields beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` affects guideline content only — the P0 field set is fixed; (b) cloudmanager release timing (same vs. subsequent release) is scheduling only — E002 is independently schedulable, and the CI lint rule is scoped to manager-binary paths until the cloudmanager conversion ships if it is deferred.\n88\t- **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Documentation Support, and the other prerequisites are all marked \"Pending human review\" in the strategy — external process gates outside this decomposition's control.\n89\t- **External adoption risk**: COO team (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) adoption is voluntary per platform policy overlay 0008; no epic is created for COO-team work because it is outside strategy control. Publishing the convention and filing tracking issues in the COO projects are in the guideline epic's scope; adoption itself is best-effort.\n90\t- **Component mapping (non-obvious)**: the strategy's affected component `rhods-operator` is not in the canonical RHAI component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`). Closest canonical name for the RHOAI platform operator codebase is **AI Core Platform**, assigned to all three code epics — all are rhods-operator repository work owned by the ODH Operator team (E002 spans the multi-cloud infrastructure sub-system of the same repository, but the most-relevant canonical component remains the operator's own). The guideline epic uses canonical component **Documentation**.\n91\t\n92\t## Tiered Delivery\n93\t\n94\tNot applicable. The only external dependency (COO team adoption) is voluntary, ungated by this strategy, and has no downstream work depending on it — there is no Tier 1/Tier 2 split. The controller-runtime logr dependency is internal and already exists.\n95\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97a05a4ae9ca92f5","agent_id":"abebec9c24d08fcdf","session_id":"cd28af09-5096-4047-9ddc-e7a6fc26f678","uuid":"42c937ab-6e00-405b-b5bc-8804b9fd9d39","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:43:44.958Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a166d693c8a10253","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
