{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0c4ac18f453d72f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E004\n3\ttitle: Document and publish the structured reconciliation log convention\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: Documentation\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n14\tai_signals:\n15\t  change_specificity: 1\n16\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n17\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n18\t  existing_foundation: 0\n19\t  open_questions: -1\n20\t  external_dependency: 0\n21\t  human_process_gates: -1\n22\t  repo_access: 1\n23\t  architecture_claims: 1\n24\tinvestigation_signals: null\n25\tai_implementability: Medium\n26\tai_implementability_score: 1\n27\tjira_key: null\n28\tbranch: null\n29\tgated_by: null\n30\tgate_failure_impact: null\n31\t---\n32\t# Document and publish the structured reconciliation log convention\n33\t\n34\t## Description\n35\t\n36\tAuthors the structured logging convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development in the rhods-operator, and publishes it as a specification for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) per platform policy (overlay 0008: RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies; the RFE approval comment confirms this approach).\n37\t\n38\t## Scope\n39\t\n40\tContent outline:\n41\t\n42\t- Required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) and the camelCase naming rationale (alignment with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions)\n43\t- The three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names, missing `resourceKind`\n44\t- Before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs\n45\t- DSCI key-name migration guide with before/after query examples for operators using the old `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` field names, covering the single-release transition called for by the strategy's risk mitigation\n46\t- The resolved decision — recorded per the open PM/Observability question — on whether the convention includes fields beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category)\n47\t- Release-notes coverage of the DSCI key migration\n48\t- Publication of the convention for external operator adoption, and tracking issues filed in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t1. The contribution guideline exists and specifies the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis.\n53\t2. The guideline includes before/after code examples for the message-embedded-identity conversion and a DSCI key migration guide with before/after query examples.\n54\t3. The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented logging behavior — a reviewer confirms the documented examples and field names match the shipped code.\n55\t4. The convention is published for external operator adoption, and tracking issues are filed in the COO Loki Operator and Tempo Operator projects.\n56\t5. The guideline records the resolved decision on optional fields beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind`.\n57\t\n58\t## Risks / Notes\n59\t\n60\t- COO team adoption is voluntary and best-effort per platform policy; filing tracking issues is in scope, but their prioritization is outside this strategy's control.\n61\t- The strategy's Documentation Support prerequisite is pending human review; the technical review acceptance criterion covers accuracy against the implementation.\n62\t\n63\t## HLR Traceability\n64\t\n65\t- HLR-8 (P1): convention documented as a contribution guideline for new controller development and published for external operator adoption\n66\t"}]},