{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b462dd1c9c2300ac","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\tcontract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8010` exited 0 — epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, re-split trigger recording, and HLR-7 `bundled` matrix flag all checked mechanically). No conditional branch files exist. Triage is `null` (full decomposition) with 4 epics — no triage/epic-count contradiction.\n15\t\n16\t## Criterion Details\n17\t\n18\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n19\tAll 8 HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 → E001+E002 (with E003 providing the \"measured by\" enforcement, correctly noted in the matrix Notes column rather than as a covering epic), HLR-2/3/4/6 → E001, HLR-5 → E002, HLR-7 → E001 (bundled), HLR-8 → E004. The single P1-in-P0 bundle (HLR-7 in E001) is not priority collapse: adding `resourceKind` to the DSC and 8 component controllers edits the same `log.Error` call sites as the P0 name/namespace conversion — incidental polish on the same mechanical pass, not a deferrable feature — and it is properly recorded with a `bundled` flag and rationale in both the matrix row and E001's HLR Traceability. Priority inheritance holds: E003 (P1) is blocked by P0 epics, never the reverse; E004 is docs-authoring and exempt (Major → P1 matches the Jira priority rule).\n20\t\n21\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n22\tFrontmatter `dependencies` match the summary diagram edge-for-edge (E001→E003, E002→E003, E001→E004, E002→E004, E003→E004), arrows drawn dependency→dependent per convention, no cycles, no invalid edges. Rule 11 is satisfied: the docs-authoring epic (E004) is blocked by all three Implementation epics. The E001/E002→E003 edges are justified Rule 13 state ordering — the repo-wide lint gate passes only on a converted codebase, so landing it earlier would fail CI on unconverted paths; E001∥E002 parallelism is correct (same repo, disjoint binaries). No konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is not implicated. Critical path is 3 against the S-strategy heuristic of 1–2, but the length is rule-driven, not a defect: Rule 11 mandates E003→E004 (docs last) and the E001→E003 edge is the justified state-ordering edge above — no unjustified serialization exists. Cross-checked epic content for uncaptured data/artifact dependencies: none found (the lint rule's inputs are the converted codebase, already edged; the guideline documents the shipped behavior, already edged).\n23\t\n24\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n25\tComponents are canonical (`AI Core Platform` for the three code epics, `Documentation` for the guideline epic — both in `.context/rhai-components.txt`), with the non-obvious rhods-operator → AI Core Platform mapping explicitly documented in Health Warnings. Single team (ODH Operator team) throughout, split by binary/work-stream rather than team — legitimate. E001 is kept above the ~2-week guideline (~1.25–1.5 sprints) as one logical change WITH a concrete recorded re-split trigger (controller-tier split if the audit finds materially more call sites or effort exceeds estimate) — a managed trade-off that counts as within guideline. No scope duplication between E001 and E002 (disjoint binaries/controllers). No work-product mixing: content authoring is isolated in E004; E001–E003 are code/CI work.\n26\t\n27\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n28\tNo Investigation epics — and the summary's Investigation Epic Analysis is correct that none is warranted: each open question either changes effort only (audit percentage → folded into E001 scope/ACs), is resolved by work inside an epic (alert-rule inventory → backwards-compat ACs), is a